# SPEC GOLDEN — hand-derived from css-flexbox-1 §9.5 before the code existed,
# never regenerated. The overflow case: what alignment does when there is no
# free space to align *with*.
#
# Three `flex: 0 0 320px` (= 40 cell) items in an 80-cell container. Grow and
# shrink are both 0, so §9.7 hands each item its 40 cells and the line needs
# 120: free space is -40.
#
# Negative free space is not distributed. §9.5 step 1 only fires "if the
# remaining free space is positive", so auto margins are zero, and every
# packing value here falls back to `flex-start`.
#
# That last part is a deliberate departure, and this golden is where it is
# written down. css-align-3 §9.3 only specifies the `flex-start` fallback for
# `space-between`; `space-around` and `space-evenly` fall back to `center`, and
# `center` is *unsafe* by default — a browser lets an overflowing row hang off
# both ends. This engine does what `safe center` does instead. The reason is
# the medium: a browser's start-edge overflow is recoverable, because the
# reader can scroll left to it, and a terminal has no negative column. The
# first item would not be clipped, it would be gone.
#
# Row 1 — `justify-content: center`, packed from main-start anyway:
#   x = 0, 40, 80. The third item is entirely past the right edge, which is
#   what "overflow" means; painting clips it to the screen.
#
# Row 2 — `justify-content: space-between`, likewise: x = 0, 40, 80. With
#   negative free space there is nothing to put between them.
#
# Row 3 — the same overflow under `row-reverse`, where main-start is the right
#   edge. **Rewritten in M9.10's review, and the reason is two paragraphs up.**
#
#   This row used to read x = 40, 0, -40: main-start was taken to be the
#   container's right edge whatever the content did, so the line was measured
#   back from 80 and the third item landed at a negative column. That is
#   precisely the outcome the `safe center` paragraph above rejects — an item
#   before the near edge is not clipped, it is gone, and no amount of scrolling
#   reaches a column that does not exist — so the file argued for one rule on
#   the forward axis and recorded its opposite on the reversed one.
#
#   A reversed axis now counts back from the far edge of the *content* when the
#   content overflows, which is the container's far edge whenever it fits and
#   so changes nothing anywhere else. The line needs 120 cells, so:
#   a: 120 - 0 - 40 = 80,  b: 120 - 40 - 40 = 40,  c: 120 - 80 - 40 = 0.
#   The reversed order is intact — a is still the rightmost item — and the
#   overflow runs off the same edge every other overflow in the engine runs
#   off, where paint clips it (M9.3). What it costs is that the item left
#   on screen is the last one rather than the first; what it buys is that no
#   stage downstream of layout is ever handed a negative coordinate.
<html>  x=0 y=0 w=80 h=3
  <body>  x=0 y=0 w=80 h=3
    <div.row> flex row  x=0 y=0 w=80 h=1
      <div>  x=0 y=0 w=40 h=1
        anonymous  x=0 y=0 w=40 h=1
          line  x=0 y=0 w=40 h=1
            #text "a"  x=0 y=0 w=1 h=1
      <div>  x=40 y=0 w=40 h=1
        anonymous  x=40 y=0 w=40 h=1
          line  x=40 y=0 w=40 h=1
            #text "b"  x=40 y=0 w=1 h=1
      <div>  x=80 y=0 w=40 h=1
        anonymous  x=80 y=0 w=40 h=1
          line  x=80 y=0 w=40 h=1
            #text "c"  x=80 y=0 w=1 h=1
    <div.row.between> flex row  x=0 y=1 w=80 h=1
      <div>  x=0 y=1 w=40 h=1
        anonymous  x=0 y=1 w=40 h=1
          line  x=0 y=1 w=40 h=1
            #text "a"  x=0 y=1 w=1 h=1
      <div>  x=40 y=1 w=40 h=1
        anonymous  x=40 y=1 w=40 h=1
          line  x=40 y=1 w=40 h=1
            #text "b"  x=40 y=1 w=1 h=1
      <div>  x=80 y=1 w=40 h=1
        anonymous  x=80 y=1 w=40 h=1
          line  x=80 y=1 w=40 h=1
            #text "c"  x=80 y=1 w=1 h=1
    <div.row.rev> flex row-reverse  x=0 y=2 w=80 h=1
      <div>  x=80 y=2 w=40 h=1
        anonymous  x=80 y=2 w=40 h=1
          line  x=80 y=2 w=40 h=1
            #text "a"  x=80 y=2 w=1 h=1
      <div>  x=40 y=2 w=40 h=1
        anonymous  x=40 y=2 w=40 h=1
          line  x=40 y=2 w=40 h=1
            #text "b"  x=40 y=2 w=1 h=1
      <div>  x=0 y=2 w=40 h=1
        anonymous  x=0 y=2 w=40 h=1
          line  x=0 y=2 w=40 h=1
            #text "c"  x=0 y=2 w=1 h=1
